## Acceptance

- Exact baseline source plus emitted transform reproduces the local oracle
  byte-for-byte.
- Comments and formatting may be ignored for source identity without being
  silently discarded from preserved output/provenance.
- Content/token anchors tolerate non-semantic insertions better than absolute
  byte
  offsets.
- Identity, compatibility, and bug probes have separate semantics.
- An upstream source that already satisfies a bug-fix postcondition can remain
  compatible without reproducing the original bug.
- Generated transform without enough admitted source cannot materialize the
  target.
- Unrelated source and behavior-only clones fail closed.
- Source-binding failure occurs before accepted target files are published.
- Generic tests contain no third-party DOOM source.
- Generation and exact-baseline materialization are deterministic and
  byte-identical across repeated runs.
- Threshold values are justified by calibration evidence and documented as
  technical policy rather than legal conclusions.
- Jig repository validation passes without weakening project governance.

## Evidence

- Expected durable artifact surface: `algorithms/diff/`,
  `docs/technical/tooling/source-bound-diff-generator.md`, and synthetic tests.
- First integration consumer:
  `src/research/algorithms/domain/algorithms/doom/generator/quality.py`.
- Second integration consumer:
  `src/research/algorithms/domain/algorithms/doom/generator/amalgamate.py`,
  which emits the canonical source-bound single-file C transformation.
- Required evidence: exact reconstruction hashes, threshold-boundary fixtures,
  wrong/no-source rejection, behavior-probe fixtures, deterministic generation,
  source-binding negative tests, and `jig validate --root .`.
